        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12463515/comment/12863154",
          "id": "12863154",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=rcmuir",
            "name": "rcmuir",
            "key": "rcmuir",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150"
            },
            "displayName": "Robert Muir",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "body": "I think most apps will be unaffected by this change (if the prefix-flex index convertor can sort the terms in binary, too).\n\nBut we need to lookout for some traps:\n* Things that use String.compareTo are dangerous, as it uses code unit order (e.g. i see a binary search w/ this in FieldCache)\n* In general assuming a term can be a String at all is problematic with using byte[] terms, if numeric wants to use full byte, etc. \nSo we should think about changing Term, too.\n\nthe best way to avoid problems is to stick with byte[] as much as possible and try to avoid using String for terms... ",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=rcmuir",
            "name": "rcmuir",
            "key": "rcmuir",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=rcmuir&avatarId=10150",
